        "phase": "synthesis",
        "plan": {
          "A": "Research an exact sparse-obstacle alternative rather than further tuning the slower four-row sweep. Sort and deduplicate blockers; handle blocked endpoints correctly. For each blocker and then the destination, compute unrestricted binomial paths minus contributions whose first blocked point was an earlier coordinate-wise predecessor: w_i = C(y_i+x_i,y_i) - sum_j w_j*C(y_i-y_j+x_i-x_j,y_i-y_j), modulo 1000000007. Precompute factorials and inverse factorials through 2N-2, which is below the modulus; use wide products and normalized subtraction. This gives O(N + U log U + U²) work for U unique blockers, including the general obstacle-free binomial case. Retain a dense-DP fallback: the stated density bound does not guarantee small U. Investigate a conservative, overflow-safe cost-based crossover, never recognition of benchmark inputs. Deliver this as a separately measurable candidate; its benefit on this run is not yet established.",
          "B": "Own the strongest measured dense backend, starting from 4e4442b510654792. Preserve descending in-place anti-diagonal updates, reuse of original neighboring NEON vectors, and obstacle clearing before the next diagonal. Keep every residue normalized; then sums are below 2*MOD and unsigned minimum with sum-MOD is exact. Investigate bounded unroll and load-scheduling alternatives against the retained sixteen-cell version, with correct scalar tails and a portable fallback. Separately measure unsynchronized input and flat anti-diagonal CSR obstacle storage, adapting C's compact grouping without its unnecessary row sorting: repeated obstacle clears are harmless. Preserve padding and shrinking-diagonal boundary invariants. Retain 7322070c36404ba1 as a two-buffer control; the measured single-buffer advantage is small. Do not parallelize the descending buffer directly, since concurrent writes can destroy another worker's preceding-diagonal inputs.",
          "C": "Own the four-core parallel alternative, beginning with stripe-pipeline candidate 6e041d7b3da64157 rather than the slower barrier-based tiled version. Research NEON inclusive-prefix updates inside obstacle-free row runs: perform modular correction after each scan stage, add the incoming left carry modulo MOD, and reset the carry at blockers. This attacks the remaining scalar dependency while preserving the existing stripe ownership and immutable boundary exchange. Maintain padded release/acquire progress counters, actual-team-size handling, bounded synchronization batches, and serial fallback when useful work cannot amortize parallel overhead. Compare this hybrid against both the original stripe program and B, not merely against the baseline. Respect the existing four-core target and build configuration. Reject variants requiring a barrier per cell anti-diagonal or unsafe concurrent use of B's in-place buffer.",
          "G": "Use 4e4442b510654792 as the integration anchor and incumbent. First measure the isolated transfer of unsynchronized, untied input from A/C; then independently assess compact anti-diagonal obstacle storage. All candidates modify the same file, so integrate invariants and transformations deliberately rather than stacking patches. Obtain complete candidate artifacts before integration; the supplied stripe diff is truncated. Consider A's sparse dispatcher and C's parallel backend only after their complete programs are validated and measured. Dispatch may depend on general input size, unique blocker count, and supported execution capabilities, never test identities or expected answers. Measure each combination and the final whole program using the unchanged harness and budget; retain the fastest validated complete program even if it contains only B. Audit N=1, blocked endpoints, unsorted/repeated blockers, boundary obstacles, SIMD tails, modular normalization, and concurrent ownership. Preserve the single-integer output and all valid inputs; leave tests, oracle, compiler, target, permissions, and budgets unchanged.",
          "analysis": "The supplied source implements Problem D; A/B/C are research-role labels. Baseline time is 80.379 ms. B's descending-buffer NEON wavefront leads at 9.409 ms, about 8.54x faster, versus 9.614 ms for its two-buffer predecessor. C's stripe and tiled variants take 10.833 and 11.525 ms; A's skewed and scalar variants take 17.197 and 22.697 ms. Thus SIMD wavefront execution is the strongest measured foundation, while additional parallelism has not yet beaten it. The 2.1% difference between B variants warrants caution without variability data. Input/storage overhead offers a low-risk integration hypothesis; sparse combinatorics offers an unmeasured asymptotic alternative for sufficiently few blockers. No integration measurements are supplied, so gains must not be treated as additive."
        },
