            "B": "Research a separate parallel version of the same exact sparse recurrence rather than a speculative approximation. Group blocked points by x+y: a dominated predecessor always has a strictly smaller diagonal, so all outputs within one diagonal can safely compute concurrently from completed earlier diagonals. Give each worker a private 64-bit accumulation and reduce modulo MOD; avoid parallel overhead for small groups and retain a serial fallback. Measure whether four ARM64 cores overcome the recurrence's memory traffic and synchronization. Preserve deduplication and all endpoint handling; do not assume obstacle density, input ordering, or benchmark values.",
            "C": "Produce and assess an exact dense/hybrid fallback for regimes where O(M^2) inclusion-exclusion loses. Replace vector<vector<bool>>(n,n) with sorted obstacle columns per row (O(M+n) metadata) and retain only rolling O(n) DP rows; process open row segments without an n-by-n blocked allocation. Consider a carefully validated segmented-prefix implementation for row parallelism, but keep a low-overhead serial rolling implementation as the dense reference. Establish a measured, input-shape-based crossover between dense O(n^2) work and sparse O(M^2) work; no fixed-test specialization. Also compare every candidate against the supplied semantics for coordinates and modulo arithmetic.",
            "G": "Integrate only a candidate that is both correct and faster under the existing build/run conditions; the observed correct baseline is already 0.080853 s, so combined changes may regress. Prefer the sparse combinatorial path for its asymptotic elimination of the current n^2 storage/work, with a measured dense fallback only if C demonstrates a real crossover. Preserve stdin/stdout exactly, print one modular integer and newline, use the existing compiler/script/budgets unchanged, and validate randomized small cases against the original DP plus boundary cases. Do not blindly combine parallel and hybrid changes: retain the fastest validated complete implementation for every valid input.",
            "analysis": "The active source is Problem D, not A-C: it counts monotone paths on an n-by-n grid with blocked cells. Its rolling DP still allocates blockedCells at O(n^2) and executes O(n^2) recurrence steps, which is incompatible with the stated n bound even when obstacles are sparse. The key exact optimization is path-count inclusion-exclusion over blocked cells using binomial coefficients modulo 1,000,000,007; it has O(n+M^2) time and O(n+M) memory, while a dense rolling-row fallback costs O(n^2) time and O(n+M) memory. The only automatic timing is a correct 0.080853 s baseline over three samples, so all proposals must be measured independently and none should be presumed additive."
