    "mother_plans": {
      "mother_plan:preparation": {
        "model_calls": 1,
        "phase": "preparation",
        "plan": {
          "A": "Research an O(N²) serial algorithm to replace the baseline’s O(SN²) candidate updates: keep per-column totals for the current S-row band, scan horizontal S-column windows using a rolling sum, then update the column totals by removing the exiting row and adding the entering row. Use signed 64-bit intermediates; retain full generality for negative matrix values and all legal S. Compare correctness and end-to-end time with the baseline.",
          "B": "Isolate data-path improvements without changing the algorithm initially: benchmark contiguous row-major storage instead of vector-of-vectors and buffered integer input instead of mixed C++/C input. Preserve standard whitespace and signed-integer parsing. Attribute gains only from measured end-to-end runs, and retain changes only if all official cases remain correct and time improves.",
          "C": "Explore an alternative O(N²) summed-area-table implementation that makes candidate-window evaluation independent across rows and can use the available 4 CPU cores if supported by the existing build configuration. Keep prefix values and window arithmetic in signed 64-bit, consider the memory cost before choosing this route, and measure parallel overhead and total runtime; do not change compiler selection or target.",
          "G": "Integrate only candidate programs produced by A, B, and C. Validate each and every combination against the official correctness checks, then measure full end-to-end runtime; gains may not add, so compare combinations directly rather than assuming a faster component improves the whole. Keep the best measured correct candidate (or the baseline if none wins). Do not specialize to observed test values or alter tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, permissions, or source outside the candidate work.",
          "analysis": "The supplied source solves the maximum S×S sum with a serpentine sliding window, updating S cells per move (O(SN²)); it stores the matrix as nested vectors. The baseline is correct on 4 cases, with three measured times 0.124624, 0.126576, and 0.124979 seconds (mean 0.125393 seconds) and reported score 0.5. No candidate or interaction measurements are available, and the cases do not establish input sizes or bottlenecks. The problem permits any 0<S<N<8000 and guarantees only that the maximum result fits a signed 32-bit integer, so use wider intermediates and preserve correctness for arbitrary signed entries. The target is ARM64 with 4 CPU cores and no GPUs."
        },
