      "mother_plan:synthesis": {
        "model_calls": 1,
        "phase": "synthesis",
        "plan": {
          "A": "Explore scalar four-target unrolling of the force kernel on a branch using the best persistent-OpenMP implementation as its comparison base. The prior scalar-unroll candidate was modestly faster than baseline (0.60008 s vs. 0.66054 s), but do not add OpenMP SIMD: the tiled SIMD candidate measured 0.21493 s, slower than the best 0.17020 s. Preserve each target’s ascending source-body accumulation order, handle any remainder, and benchmark the complete program.",
          "B": "Investigate whether position-only structure-of-arrays storage improves the force kernel when paired with persistent OpenMP. Prior full-SoA candidates without OpenMP measured about baseline (0.66061 s and 0.66122 s), so treat this as a new hypothesis, not an established gain. Include packing and output reconstruction costs; preserve the binary Body input/output format, arithmetic, and source traversal order. Do not repeat an ivdep-only change, which also showed no measurable gain.",
          "C": "Use the measured 0.17020 s candidate as the lead: one OpenMP parallel region across iterations, static worksharing over independent target bodies and integration, and a generic small-input serial fallback. Refine only general scheduling or threshold choices for the four-core ARM64 target. Preserve the force-to-position-update barrier and per-target accumulation order; the tiled SIMD alternative measured slower at 0.21493 s.",
          "G": "Treat the 0.17020 s result as a performance lead, not a correctness-certified winner: the supplied explicit correctness result is for baseline only. Verify each candidate against the unchanged oracle on all cases, including binary I/O and the full simulation. Benchmark complete integrated programs; add one change at a time and retain it only if the integrated result is correct and measurably faster. Do not assume gains from separate candidates add together.",
          "analysis": "The task is the N-body simulator in src/nbody.c. The baseline is explicitly correct and measures 0.66054 s. The fastest observed candidate is persistent OpenMP at 0.17020 s, about 3.9x faster; another OpenMP candidate with four-target SIMD tiles measures 0.21493 s. The scalar-unroll candidate is modestly faster than baseline, while prior SoA and ivdep-only candidates are effectively unchanged. The target is ARM64 with four CPU cores and no GPU, supporting CPU parallelism rather than GPU work. Keep all experiments general and preserve correctness for every valid input; supplied candidate timings do not by themselves establish correctness."
