    "mother_plans": {
      "mother_plan:preparation": {
        "model_calls": 1,
        "phase": "preparation",
        "plan": {
          "A": "Research a matrix-free Kitamasa candidate. Use the recurrence polynomial x^N - P1*x^(N-1) - ... - PN and compute x^(H-1) modulo it; combine the resulting coefficients with A1..AN. Target O(N^2 log H) work and O(N) storage instead of dense O(N^3 log(H-N)) matrix powers. Check indexing and modular arithmetic carefully, including N=1, and benchmark against the existing implementation.",
          "B": "Research a short-gap path for d=H-N: generate the d new terms directly from the last N values using a rolling buffer, with each term computed from the full weighted recurrence. This costs O(N*d), so compare it with the matrix method and use it only for a generally applicable, measured crossover; retain a correct fallback for larger gaps. Do not specialize to sample values.",
          "C": "Explore Bostan-Mori as an independent recurrence-evaluation candidate. Form the generating function for F1..FN with denominator 1-P1*z-...-PN*z^N, then extract F_H using index H-1. Its polynomial products are O(N^2) per bit; evaluate parallelizing independent output coefficients on the 4-core ARM64 target, with a serial path if parallel overhead loses. Normalize signed intermediate values modulo 1000000007 and verify indexing against the baseline.",
          "G": "Integrate only candidates that pass correctness and improve measured end-to-end runtime. Compare each tree's result and any combined dispatcher against the unchanged baseline and full existing correctness suite; gains may not add. Any dispatch must depend on general input/work characteristics, preserve correctness for all valid inputs, and avoid sample-specific answers. Keep compiler, tests, oracle, budgets, and target unchanged.",
          "analysis": "The source uses dense 1022-by-1022 matrix storage and generic cubic multiplication with a modulo operation for each multiply-add; its power loop also squares after the last needed bit, and the final base matrix has only its first column populated. These provide algorithmic and constant-factor opportunities. The run's baseline is correct on 4 cases, with mean 0.019866 s (samples 0.019786, 0.019921, 0.019892 s) and score 0.5. This is a small measured workload with no reported size distribution, so speedups—especially parallel ones—must be demonstrated rather than presumed. Target: ARM64, 4 CPU cores, no GPU."
