    "mother_plans": {
      "mother_plan:preparation": {
        "model_calls": 1,
        "phase": "preparation",
        "plan": {
          "A": "Research output buffering in src/permutations.cc while retaining lexicographic enumeration. Compare replacing std::endl with newline against constructing output directly in bounded byte buffers and issuing large writes. Remove per-character formatted stream operations; investigate disabling unnecessary stream synchronization without mixing incompatible output APIs. Try only a small set of buffer sizes within the existing evaluation budget. Preserve every output byte, flush the final partial buffer, and support rows larger than the buffer through a safe streaming fallback. Do not accumulate all permutations. Deliver the best correct complete candidate with automatic end-to-end measurements under the unchanged build and evaluator.",
          "B": "Research fused permutation generation and formatting. Compare std::next_permutation on the sorted input plus a reusable formatted record against a successor algorithm operating directly on that record's character slots. Fixed brackets, spaces and newline can remain initialized; swaps and suffix reversals need touch only character positions before copying the record into bounded output batches. Ensure the successor scan never forms an iterator before begin, including the final descending permutation. Preserve native char comparison and duplicate suppression; avoid alphabet restrictions or factorial-sized storage. Check record-size arithmetic and retain a streaming fallback for oversized records. Measure whether reduced formatting outweighs strided access and copying, using the unchanged build and evaluator.",
          "C": "Research ordered CPU parallelism, only if threading is supported by the fixed build. Partition the runtime multiset into distinct lexicographically ordered prefix subtrees; each task enumerates its remaining sorted suffix. Adaptively splitting prefixes can expose work despite repeated symbols without computing overflowing factorials. Use at most four execution threads total, with one ordered output writer and bounded producer buffers. Scheduling and backpressure must guarantee that later tasks cannot prevent the earliest unfinished task from progressing. Never retain a complete large subtree's output. Investigate a serial fallback based on runtime workload and repetition, not particular test values; use checked or saturating arithmetic for estimates. Compare serial and parallel versions end-to-end under the unchanged evaluator, including startup and synchronization. If threading is unavailable or slower, retain the serial candidate rather than changing build settings.",
          "G": "Integrate measured programs, not presumed independent speedups. Retain the best correct standalone candidate, then evaluate compatible combinations: A's buffered writer with B's generator/record representation, and C's ordered producer pipeline only if it improves the complete program. Resolve competing buffer ownership and output implementations explicitly rather than merging them mechanically. Validate each integration with the unchanged evaluator and compare available timing samples against both its inputs and the current best; reject regressions and treat small differences cautiously. Audit empty and whitespace-only input, singleton and repeated symbols, final-permutation termination, native char ordering, overflow, buffer boundaries and parallel output ordering. Every required row must actually reach stdout. Keep tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target and permissions unchanged; deliver the fastest demonstrated correct complete program.",
          "analysis": "The active task is Problem D, implemented in src/permutations.cc. Shared contract: read through EOF, remove exactly the six whitespace characters recognized by the source, preserve native char ordering, and emit every distinct permutation in lexicographic order as \"[ \" followed by each character and a space, then \"]\\n\". Empty filtered input emits nothing. For nonempty length n and P distinct permutations, output requires P(2n+4) bytes, so exhaustive output cannot be bypassed. The baseline passed four cases and averaged 0.132437 seconds, with samples spanning 0.131519–0.133778 seconds. There is no profile or workload-size evidence. Per-character stream insertion and per-row flushing are therefore strong optimization hypotheses, not measured bottlenecks. Prioritize buffering, then generation/formatting fusion; investigate parallelism cautiously on the four-core ARM64 CPU because output serialization and short runtimes may erase its benefits."
        },
