    "mother_plans": {
      "mother_plan:preparation": {
        "model_calls": 1,
        "phase": "preparation",
        "plan": {
          "A": "Build the conservative multicore candidate: retain the AoS representation and the exact scalar inner j loop, but parallelize independent outer i work with static OpenMP scheduling. Use one persistent 4-thread parallel region across the 10 force/integration iterations, retaining barriers between force and position phases. Do not use j reductions, fast-math, sqrtf, approximations, or altered output framing. Measure against the 0.662700725 s baseline with the existing oracle.",
          "B": "Build a separate locality candidate: stage Body data into aligned SoA position/velocity arrays, run the same safe outer-i OpenMP decomposition, then pack bodies back in the reference binary layout. Preserve increasing j accumulation order, float expression order, the source's sqrt call, constants, iteration count, and the fact that output contains bodies only. Evaluate whether reduced strided loads outweigh staging overhead; report correctness and timing independently.",
          "C": "Research a strict-arithmetic microkernel candidate. Keep each body's j contributions committed in increasing order, while testing local i coordinates, bounded scalar unrolling/software pipelining, and cache tiling only when force-update timing remains identical. It may use the safe 4-thread outer decomposition, but must reject pair-symmetry, SIMD reductions, reciprocal/sqrt approximations, sqrtf, and unsafe FP flags. Return only an oracle-correct measured variant.",
          "G": "Integrate empirically, not additively. Compare each standalone candidate and only controlled merges such as OpenMP plus the best layout or microkernel change using the unchanged correctness harness and repeated timing. Preserve binary I/O behavior, numerical semantics, and four-core OpenMP launch settings. Select the fastest fully correct result; discard any merge that regresses, even if its components improved alone.",
          "analysis": "The measured serial O(10*n^2) baseline is 0.662700725 s on a 4-core ARM64 CPU with no GPU. During force computation positions are read-only and each i updates only its own velocity, so outer-i parallelism is the safest main gain. Reference-compatible floating behavior is fragile: retain j order, source arithmetic including double sqrt promotion, constants, barriers, and binary protocol."
        },
        "request_sha256": "f9948b2e5d268b36926f8f2135fc7e92ffad8cbea2b5341141533535345988af",
        "response": {
          "data": {
            "A": "Build the conservative multicore candidate: retain the AoS representation and the exact scalar inner j loop, but parallelize independent outer i work with static OpenMP scheduling. Use one persistent 4-thread parallel region across the 10 force/integration iterations, retaining barriers between force and position phases. Do not use j reductions, fast-math, sqrtf, approximations, or altered output framing. Measure against the 0.662700725 s baseline with the existing oracle.",
            "B": "Build a separate locality candidate: stage Body data into aligned SoA position/velocity arrays, run the same safe outer-i OpenMP decomposition, then pack bodies back in the reference binary layout. Preserve increasing j accumulation order, float expression order, the source's sqrt call, constants, iteration count, and the fact that output contains bodies only. Evaluate whether reduced strided loads outweigh staging overhead; report correctness and timing independently.",
            "C": "Research a strict-arithmetic microkernel candidate. Keep each body's j contributions committed in increasing order, while testing local i coordinates, bounded scalar unrolling/software pipelining, and cache tiling only when force-update timing remains identical. It may use the safe 4-thread outer decomposition, but must reject pair-symmetry, SIMD reductions, reciprocal/sqrt approximations, sqrtf, and unsafe FP flags. Return only an oracle-correct measured variant.",
            "G": "Integrate empirically, not additively. Compare each standalone candidate and only controlled merges such as OpenMP plus the best layout or microkernel change using the unchanged correctness harness and repeated timing. Preserve binary I/O behavior, numerical semantics, and four-core OpenMP launch settings. Select the fastest fully correct result; discard any merge that regresses, even if its components improved alone.",
            "analysis": "The measured serial O(10*n^2) baseline is 0.662700725 s on a 4-core ARM64 CPU with no GPU. During force computation positions are read-only and each i updates only its own velocity, so outer-i parallelism is the safest main gain. Reference-compatible floating behavior is fragile: retain j order, source arithmetic including double sqrt promotion, constants, barriers, and binary protocol."
          },
