            "C": "Research structural rejection and candidate filtering. Keep size checking and the successful identity path cheap. Derive invariants from the actual Boolean adjacency matrix, not declared edge counts: loop flags, off-diagonal degrees, component signatures, then jointly computed color refinement using exact signatures and shared color identifiers across both graphs. Unequal corresponding signature/color multiplicities prove non-isomorphism; equal colors never prove isomorphism. During unchanged Heap enumeration, maintain the number of vertex mappings incompatible with the final colors, updating the two swapped positions in constant time; verify adjacency only when this count is zero. Stage preprocessing and measure whether deeper refinement repays its overhead. Deliver a standalone candidate and expose compatibility information for A's block pruning.",
            "G": "Integrate programs actually produced by A/B/C, starting from the best independently measured candidate. Add compatible changes incrementally and retain the fastest validated standalone or combination; gains are not assumed additive. In particular, skipped Heap blocks must leave the permutation, color counters and any verification metadata consistent; cached witnesses should be rechecked against the resulting mapping. Audit identity-only output, nonidentity multiple-match ordering, no-match output, unequal sizes, self-loops and duplicate-edge collapse. Validate and time through the unchanged evaluator within existing budgets, considering the baseline's millisecond-scale variation. Prefer portable, low-overhead CPU implementations over unmeasured threading. Do not change tests, oracle, compiler, target, permissions or budgets, and never specialize to measured inputs or expected answers.",
