    "mother_plans": {
      "mother_plan:preparation": {
        "model_calls": 1,
        "phase": "preparation",
        "plan": {
          "A": "Research successor-generation optimizations while retaining the existing BFS and visited-table structure. Precompute input-derived neighbor and push-destination tables with explicit boundary checks, avoiding repeated coordinate division. Compare the current box scans against a reusable occupancy map built once per expanded state. Boxes are already canonicalized: skip sorting after ordinary walks, and replace bubble sorting after pushes with removal/reinsertion of the single changed coordinate. For walk successors, evaluate updating the existing unsigned polynomial hash using the player-coordinate change and precomputed 31^n_boxes; retain full recomputation after pushes initially. Preserve exact candidate order and canonical keys. Measure these changes separately: occupancy-map setup or extra tables may cost more than they save on small puzzles.",
          "B": "Research exact visited-state storage and allocation, keeping successor legality unchanged. Begin with the inexpensive cached-hash comparison before memcmp in lookup. Then evaluate deterministic hash mixing for bucket selection and an open-addressed table holding stable state indices, always resolving collisions with complete player-and-box key equality. Construct candidate keys in reusable scratch storage and allocate permanent records only for first visits. Evaluate a correctly aligned arena with compact parent handles and separate frontier vectors, avoiding intrusive chaining and queue pointers. Arena growth must not invalidate references. Frontier vectors must reproduce the current reverse-discovery layer traversal, including immediate goal checks and first-discovery parent retention; ordinary FIFO replacement is not equivalent. Benchmark hash filtering, table replacement, and compact storage independently before combining them.",
          "C": "Research sound deadlock pruning without replacing unit-move BFS. Start with a cheap local check after pushes: a completely wall/box-filled 2x2 block containing an off-goal box is permanently frozen. Next evaluate per-goal reverse-push reachability, including the required player-support square, extending the existing union-valued live map. Build the necessary box-to-goal compatibility graph and reject configurations lacking an injective assignment of every box to a distinct reachable goal. Do not assume equal box and goal counts. Evaluate initially and after pushes; ordinary walks can reuse the box-configuration result. Any cache must verify exact sorted box keys. Preserve surviving-state order and never reject solved configurations. Avoid speculative deadlock rules, push-count optimization, or player-region merging. Retain only pruning variants whose reduced search outweighs their preprocessing, matching, and cache costs.",
          "G": "Select the fastest valid standalone child program before integrating features. Integrate incrementally within the unchanged evaluation budget, measuring each combination rather than assuming additive gains. Reconcile cached-hash definitions, canonical box ordering, storage handles, alignment, and frontier ordering explicitly; A's incremental hash must match B's chosen representation. Validate complete output against the unchanged oracle, not merely solution legality or length, and review correctness beyond the four observed cases. Keep structures input-sized, collision checks exact, and any bounded fast path guarded by a general fallback. Use the existing timing protocol and report variability; the baseline samples span approximately 0.300 ms. Reject regressions, including pruning or compact-table combinations that lose their standalone gains. Do not change the compiler, target, tests, permissions, or budgets. Retain the baseline if no defensible improvement survives integration.",
          "analysis": "The relevant program is Sokoban's unit-player-move BFS over the player position and sorted box positions. Plausible costs are repeated box scans, copying/sorting/hashing successors, chained visited lookups, and pointer-heavy state storage; no profile establishes their relative importance. This run's baseline passed four correctness cases and averaged 4.982 ms across three samples, ranging from 4.816 to 5.116 ms. Consequently, favor low-overhead sequential experiments on the four-core ARM64 target rather than assuming threading will help. Preserve shortest total player movement, exact state identity, and deterministic first-parent behavior. In particular, the source generates right, left, up, down but prepends each next-layer frontier; switching to conventional FIFO up/down/left/right traversal can change the oracle-visible path. All proposed changes must remain general across valid inputs, not encode measured boards or expected solutions."
        },
        "request_sha256": "2e25d0792386c5333a438cf1b12716761a5502685860a83d26262e509e95dd7b",
