            "C": "Research sound deadlock pruning without replacing unit-move BFS. Start with a cheap local check after pushes: a completely wall/box-filled 2x2 block containing an off-goal box is permanently frozen. Next evaluate per-goal reverse-push reachability, including the required player-support square, extending the existing union-valued live map. Build the necessary box-to-goal compatibility graph and reject configurations lacking an injective assignment of every box to a distinct reachable goal. Do not assume equal box and goal counts. Evaluate initially and after pushes; ordinary walks can reuse the box-configuration result. Any cache must verify exact sorted box keys. Preserve surviving-state order and never reject solved configurations. Avoid speculative deadlock rules, push-count optimization, or player-region merging. Retain only pruning variants whose reduced search outweighs their preprocessing, matching, and cache costs.",
            "G": "Select the fastest valid standalone child program before integrating features. Integrate incrementally within the unchanged evaluation budget, measuring each combination rather than assuming additive gains. Reconcile cached-hash definitions, canonical box ordering, storage handles, alignment, and frontier ordering explicitly; A's incremental hash must match B's chosen representation. Validate complete output against the unchanged oracle, not merely solution legality or length, and review correctness beyond the four observed cases. Keep structures input-sized, collision checks exact, and any bounded fast path guarded by a general fallback. Use the existing timing protocol and report variability; the baseline samples span approximately 0.300 ms. Reject regressions, including pruning or compact-table combinations that lose their standalone gains. Do not change the compiler, target, tests, permissions, or budgets. Retain the baseline if no defensible improvement survives integration.",
