    "mother_plans": {
      "mother_plan:preparation": {
        "model_calls": 1,
        "phase": "preparation",
        "plan": {
          "A": "Research exact serial selection, initially leaving parsing unchanged. Compare a sorted-buffer fast path with immediate rejection against the worst retained distance against an O(N log k) bounded max-heap. Heap keys must include distance and global input ordinal so later equal-distance points lose. Measure whether a small-k sorted buffer merits retention. Replace label qsort with a character-domain histogram, aggregating repeated labels and preserving native-char tie ordering. A general k==N shortcut can count all labels without computing distances. Preserve reference-compatible handling outside optimized-path preconditions; never silently clamp k. Evaluate changes incrementally with the unchanged automatic evaluator.",
          "B": "Research input throughput and storage, initially keeping baseline selection. Develop an fread-backed scanner for the actual source grammar, including n_groups=, labels, lengths, coordinates and k. Preserve accepted whitespace and numeric syntax, including signs and exponents, and handle buffer boundaries correctly. Use strtof as the float-conversion correctness baseline; avoid approximate decimal conversion or double-rounding differences from scanf %f. Evaluate contiguous point storage with compact group metadata and safe allocation arithmetic. Because k and the query arrive last, retain points or replayable input; do not assume seekable stdin or compute distances before the query is available. Measure complete-program gains, not merely scanner throughput.",
          "C": "Research an independent full-array, expected-linear selection alternative. Compute baseline-equivalent float distances, find the kth-distance cutoff using three-way introselect with a guarded fallback, then include every strictly closer point and only the required earliest equal-cutoff points. Retain original ordinals or perform an original-order pass: arbitrary tied partition prefixes are incorrect. Establish a valid serial implementation first. Then evaluate independent distance generation and integer vote counting using up to four CPU workers, only through facilities supported by the fixed build. Explore vectorization without changing per-point floating-point evaluation. Include allocation, partitioning, copies and worker startup in timings; compare against A rather than assuming linear selection or parallelism wins.",
          "G": "Integrate measured winners, not presumed additive gains. Preserve the fastest valid standalone program as a control, and selectively evaluate A+B and C+B combinations through the complete unchanged correctness and timing workflow. Audit common ordinal semantics, float parsing/evaluation, equal-distance boundaries, vote ties, duplicate labels, supported edge k values and exact one-character stdout. Preserve reference-compatible fallbacks wherever fast-path assumptions do not cover valid inputs. Any algorithm or worker-count dispatch must depend on general runtime properties such as N and k, never case identities or expected answers. Reassess serial versus parallel performance after integration; retain a standalone winner or baseline if combinations regress. Keep compiler, target, tests, oracle, budgets and permissions unchanged.",
          "analysis": "All research trees target the supplied KNN program, not the other official problems. This run provides only a correct baseline on four cases: mean 2.0003455 seconds, with samples spanning 1.9969–2.0064 seconds. There is no phase profile or input-size distribution. For N total points, the current selection can scan k entries per point and shift insertions, yielding O(Nk); scanf float conversion and label sorting are additional plausible costs, not proven bottlenecks. The target has four ARM64 CPU cores and no GPU. Preserve the original float-distance evaluation, earlier input points at equal-distance cutoffs, and the smallest label under the baseline native-char comparator for tied vote counts. Ordering-based optimizations need reference-compatible fallback handling for non-orderable distances or other unsupported edge cases. All improvements require measurements from this run."
        },
