    "mother_plans": {
      "mother_plan:preparation": {
        "model_calls": 1,
        "phase": "preparation",
        "plan": {
          "A": "Optimize the existing spanning-tree traversal without changing its search strategy: build adjacency lists for each generated tree once, then traverse with shared backtracking state (visited vertices and per-color counts) instead of repeatedly scanning `arvoreGeradora`, copying `hist`, and recounting colors. Preserve the candidate paths and output semantics, including isolated vertices and singleton paths. Measure this candidate independently.",
          "B": "Explore an independent exact solver on the input graph, avoiding enumeration of spanning trees. Enumerate simple paths from every start, update the best result at every path prefix, and use only proven-safe bounds (for example, colors reachable through unvisited vertices). Handle disconnected graphs, repeated colors, and singleton paths. Compare runtime and correctness with the baseline; keep this as a candidate rather than assuming it wins.",
          "C": "Investigate removing the repeated `is_cycle` check in `Prim_ST`: each relaxation considered joins a selected vertex to an unselected vertex, so adding it to the already-grown tree should not create a cycle. Verify that invariant against the included/excluded-edge handling before removing the check and its matrix copies/allocations. Keep the change localized and measure it independently.",
          "G": "Integrate candidate programs only after checking each against the unchanged official correctness setup. The baseline is valid on 4 cases, with mean 0.0062925 s (samples 0.0060052, 0.0062337, 0.0066387) and score 0.5; retain it as a fallback. Compare each candidate and each proposed combination with fresh measurements—do not assume gains add—and select only a fully correct measured improvement. Do not alter tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, or permissions.",
          "analysis": "The provided source solves the tropical-path objective through repeated spanning-tree generation and traversal. Its traversal repeatedly scans tree edges, copies path vectors, and recounts colors; its Prim routine also copies matrices and performs cycle searches during edge relaxation. These are source-derived optimization opportunities, not measured hotspots. This run’s baseline is correct but slower than the reference (score 0.5), and the observed mean is only about 6.3 ms on a 4-core ARM64 target with no GPU. Prioritize low-overhead CPU changes and measure them; parallel launch overhead is unlikely to be justified by this run alone. Preserve correctness for every valid input and do not specialize to the observed cases."
        },
        "request_sha256": "4edbedf1addf2e1590f48e1d422cef8123b9aaea0971fd6ee698df1f7cd488bc",
        "response": {
          "data": {
            "A": "Optimize the existing spanning-tree traversal without changing its search strategy: build adjacency lists for each generated tree once, then traverse with shared backtracking state (visited vertices and per-color counts) instead of repeatedly scanning `arvoreGeradora`, copying `hist`, and recounting colors. Preserve the candidate paths and output semantics, including isolated vertices and singleton paths. Measure this candidate independently.",
            "B": "Explore an independent exact solver on the input graph, avoiding enumeration of spanning trees. Enumerate simple paths from every start, update the best result at every path prefix, and use only proven-safe bounds (for example, colors reachable through unvisited vertices). Handle disconnected graphs, repeated colors, and singleton paths. Compare runtime and correctness with the baseline; keep this as a candidate rather than assuming it wins.",
            "C": "Investigate removing the repeated `is_cycle` check in `Prim_ST`: each relaxation considered joins a selected vertex to an unselected vertex, so adding it to the already-grown tree should not create a cycle. Verify that invariant against the included/excluded-edge handling before removing the check and its matrix copies/allocations. Keep the change localized and measure it independently.",
            "G": "Integrate candidate programs only after checking each against the unchanged official correctness setup. The baseline is valid on 4 cases, with mean 0.0062925 s (samples 0.0060052, 0.0062337, 0.0066387) and score 0.5; retain it as a fallback. Compare each candidate and each proposed combination with fresh measurements—do not assume gains add—and select only a fully correct measured improvement. Do not alter tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, or permissions.",
