    "mother_plans": {
      "mother_plan:preparation": {
        "model_calls": 1,
        "phase": "preparation",
        "plan": {
          "A": "Research the high-confidence output-I/O optimization in `src/permutations.cc`: replace per-permutation `std::endl` flushes with `\\n`, then measure whether disabling iostream synchronization and using buffered line writes helps further. Preserve the exact `[ `, character-plus-space, `]\\n` output and lexicographic order. Test each change separately, including exact-output correctness and end-to-end timing.",
          "B": "Investigate parallel enumeration only if measurements show permutation generation remains a bottleneck after buffered output. Partition work into disjoint lexicographic prefix shards and use bounded buffering with an ordered writer so output remains globally sorted; do not write concurrently to stdout or buffer the entire output. Measure end-to-end performance on the 4-core target, and reject added complexity if it does not improve total runtime.",
          "C": "Explore lower-risk sequential-path changes: compact the input while reading or in place to avoid the current whitespace-removal copy, and compare the custom successor loop with `std::next_permutation` for `std::string`. Keep the initial sort, duplicate handling, comparison semantics, and output behavior unchanged. Measure these separately; retain the current implementation if alternatives do not improve runtime.",
          "G": "Integrate only candidate changes supported by correctness checks and end-to-end measurements. Re-run the full correctness suite and compare each candidate and relevant combinations against the valid baseline; do not assume gains combine additively, especially for parallel generation plus output buffering. Preserve behavior for every valid input, including repeated symbols and empty input, and do not change tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, permissions, or protected files. Request each research role's change summary and measurement evidence before selecting an integration.",
          "analysis": "The official problem is lexicographically ordered generation of all distinct permutations. The supplied source sorts once, advances with a custom next-permutation routine, and prints each character separately; `std::endl` flushes after every permutation, making buffered output the clearest initial experiment. This run's automatic baseline is valid and correct on 4 cases, with mean time 0.13411276383946338 seconds (samples 0.1326565481722355, 0.13503717072308064, 0.13464457262307405) and score 0.5. These measurements establish a baseline but do not identify bottleneck proportions or support assuming combined improvements. All changes must preserve exact output and general-input correctness."
        },
        "request_sha256": "78a5d563d8a6154026a33cf7dd69429b42154b521a87bf097bf2b44647150495",
        "response": {
          "data": {
            "A": "Research the high-confidence output-I/O optimization in `src/permutations.cc`: replace per-permutation `std::endl` flushes with `\\n`, then measure whether disabling iostream synchronization and using buffered line writes helps further. Preserve the exact `[ `, character-plus-space, `]\\n` output and lexicographic order. Test each change separately, including exact-output correctness and end-to-end timing.",
            "B": "Investigate parallel enumeration only if measurements show permutation generation remains a bottleneck after buffered output. Partition work into disjoint lexicographic prefix shards and use bounded buffering with an ordered writer so output remains globally sorted; do not write concurrently to stdout or buffer the entire output. Measure end-to-end performance on the 4-core target, and reject added complexity if it does not improve total runtime.",
            "C": "Explore lower-risk sequential-path changes: compact the input while reading or in place to avoid the current whitespace-removal copy, and compare the custom successor loop with `std::next_permutation` for `std::string`. Keep the initial sort, duplicate handling, comparison semantics, and output behavior unchanged. Measure these separately; retain the current implementation if alternatives do not improve runtime.",
            "G": "Integrate only candidate changes supported by correctness checks and end-to-end measurements. Re-run the full correctness suite and compare each candidate and relevant combinations against the valid baseline; do not assume gains combine additively, especially for parallel generation plus output buffering. Preserve behavior for every valid input, including repeated symbols and empty input, and do not change tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, permissions, or protected files. Request each research role's change summary and measurement evidence before selecting an integration.",
