      "mother_plan:synthesis": {
        "model_calls": 1,
        "phase": "synthesis",
        "plan": {
          "A": "Focus on the output path in src/permutations.cc. Preserve whitespace stripping and the exact '[ c ... ]\\n' output, but avoid per-character ostream insertions and per-permutation std::endl flushes. Use the measured fixed 64-KiB bounded buffer (0.006757 s) as a reference; explore a small number of safe bulk-write or line-assembly variants, keeping output streaming and flushing the final partial block. Change only output handling so G can attribute any performance difference.",
          "B": "Focus on the permutation transition. Audit find_longest_non_increasing_suffix: on a descending terminal permutation its loop appears able to decrement back before first. Ensure the transition is well-defined for descending inputs and repeated characters, then compare a correct custom transition or std::next_permutation with std::reverse and a two-swap unrolled reversal. Benchmark each against the same buffered-output baseline, changing one transition detail at a time. Preserve unique lexicographic enumeration; do not parallelize the state-dependent successor walk.",
          "C": "Explore whether independent lexicographic prefix subspaces can provide useful parallelism on the 4-core ARM64 target. Distinct prefixes define ordered groups; generate suffix permutations independently, but use bounded buffering or backpressure and serialize output in prefix and within-group order. Preserve duplicate handling and avoid retaining the full factorial output or writing concurrently to shared stdout. Compare full runtime and memory with serial buffered output. The measured batched parallel formatter (0.010163 s) was slower than the 0.006757 s buffer candidate, so treat this as a hypothesis and recommend no integration if it does not win.",
          "G": "Integrate only after A, B, and C report results. The fastest listed result is 0.006686 s, versus the correct baseline at 0.134113 s; the buffer-only candidate measured 0.006757 s. The fastest candidate's diff contains both buffered output and unrolled reversal, although its transformation metadata labels only the reversal, so do not attribute its small margin or assume further gains will add. Use a correctness-checked buffered implementation as the base, measure each proposed composition end-to-end, and omit changes that do not improve the complete program. In particular, the measured batched parallel formatter and newline-only variant were slower than the buffer-only candidate.",
          "analysis": "The task is to enumerate every distinct permutation of the whitespace-stripped input string in lexicographic order, with exact bracket-and-space formatting. The source emits characters through repeated ostream insertions and uses std::endl for each permutation. Measurements strongly favor bulk buffered output: the best listed mean is about 20× faster than baseline, while a fixed 64-KiB buffer alone is nearly as fast. A line buffer and the tested parallel formatting approach were slower, so avoid assuming those strategies help. The successor transition is inherently sequential and its descending-terminal suffix scan appears to risk decrementing before the beginning; correctness across valid inputs takes priority over micro-optimization. No interaction measurements establish that independently measured changes combine beneficially."
        },
        "request_sha256": "fb5db69ec952a7ac8fde08da4b6181e62d6102fd1ad74f4445f8f9b326ab2706",
        "response": {
          "data": {
            "A": "Focus on the output path in src/permutations.cc. Preserve whitespace stripping and the exact '[ c ... ]\\n' output, but avoid per-character ostream insertions and per-permutation std::endl flushes. Use the measured fixed 64-KiB bounded buffer (0.006757 s) as a reference; explore a small number of safe bulk-write or line-assembly variants, keeping output streaming and flushing the final partial block. Change only output handling so G can attribute any performance difference.",
            "B": "Focus on the permutation transition. Audit find_longest_non_increasing_suffix: on a descending terminal permutation its loop appears able to decrement back before first. Ensure the transition is well-defined for descending inputs and repeated characters, then compare a correct custom transition or std::next_permutation with std::reverse and a two-swap unrolled reversal. Benchmark each against the same buffered-output baseline, changing one transition detail at a time. Preserve unique lexicographic enumeration; do not parallelize the state-dependent successor walk.",
            "C": "Explore whether independent lexicographic prefix subspaces can provide useful parallelism on the 4-core ARM64 target. Distinct prefixes define ordered groups; generate suffix permutations independently, but use bounded buffering or backpressure and serialize output in prefix and within-group order. Preserve duplicate handling and avoid retaining the full factorial output or writing concurrently to shared stdout. Compare full runtime and memory with serial buffered output. The measured batched parallel formatter (0.010163 s) was slower than the 0.006757 s buffer candidate, so treat this as a hypothesis and recommend no integration if it does not win.",
            "G": "Integrate only after A, B, and C report results. The fastest listed result is 0.006686 s, versus the correct baseline at 0.134113 s; the buffer-only candidate measured 0.006757 s. The fastest candidate's diff contains both buffered output and unrolled reversal, although its transformation metadata labels only the reversal, so do not attribute its small margin or assume further gains will add. Use a correctness-checked buffered implementation as the base, measure each proposed composition end-to-end, and omit changes that do not improve the complete program. In particular, the measured batched parallel formatter and newline-only variant were slower than the buffer-only candidate.",
            "analysis": "The task is to enumerate every distinct permutation of the whitespace-stripped input string in lexicographic order, with exact bracket-and-space formatting. The source emits characters through repeated ostream insertions and uses std::endl for each permutation. Measurements strongly favor bulk buffered output: the best listed mean is about 20× faster than baseline, while a fixed 64-KiB buffer alone is nearly as fast. A line buffer and the tested parallel formatting approach were slower, so avoid assuming those strategies help. The successor transition is inherently sequential and its descending-terminal suffix scan appears to risk decrementing before the beginning; correctness across valid inputs takes priority over micro-optimization. No interaction measurements establish that independently measured changes combine beneficially."
