    "mother_plans": {
      "mother_plan:preparation": {
        "model_calls": 1,
        "phase": "preparation",
        "plan": {
          "A": "Research ping-pong buffers to remove the separate interior copy pass. Swap after every executed sweep, including the sweep that first satisfies convergence, and print the active buffer. Keep the stencil arithmetic, threshold, iteration limit, and stopping behavior unchanged. Initialize both buffers’ untouched border cells consistently with the source’s initial grid, which matters for small valid sizes whose heated region reaches the border.",
          "B": "Research parallelizing the interior-row update on the available four-core ARM64 target, using the already-selected compiler/runtime configuration. Preserve a barrier before any copy or buffer swap, and combine per-thread convergence flags with a reduction equivalent to the source’s `hasError` test. Respect the harness’s thread budget and measure whether parallel overhead pays off; do not change the compiler or target.",
          "C": "Independently research a contiguous row-major allocation and vectorization-friendly stencil loop (for example, local non-aliasing row pointers). Keep the original neighbor addition order, update only the same interior cells, and retain the source’s convergence and output behavior. Do not combine this experiment with ping-pong buffers or threading in the initial candidate, so its effect can be measured separately.",
          "G": "Integrate the A, B, and C programs as measured candidates, not as assumed additive gains. Check standalone variants and useful combinations on the authorized correctness cases, including small sizes, zero or short iteration limits, and convergence. Preserve the original initialization, stopping semantics, and `%lf ` output format. Benchmark end-to-end on the specified target, retain only correct variants, and select the best measured integration even if it is a subset of the candidates.",
          "analysis": "The source performs a regular Jacobi stencil, checks convergence during the update, then makes a second interior pass to copy values; it also prints every grid value serially. This run’s baseline was correct on four cases, with mean time 0.0666652253518502 seconds and score 0.5; the target is ARM64 with four CPU cores and no GPU. These observations do not identify the dominant cost or predict an optimization’s gain. Keep every valid input correct, preserve the per-sweep arithmetic and convergence behavior, and verify end-to-end measurements rather than assuming gains combine."
        },
        "request_sha256": "2682de82e5f417ba4f8ac9f8ee388895e8000cf0806973137d4a6daa592e9cb1",
        "response": {
          "data": {
            "A": "Research ping-pong buffers to remove the separate interior copy pass. Swap after every executed sweep, including the sweep that first satisfies convergence, and print the active buffer. Keep the stencil arithmetic, threshold, iteration limit, and stopping behavior unchanged. Initialize both buffers’ untouched border cells consistently with the source’s initial grid, which matters for small valid sizes whose heated region reaches the border.",
            "B": "Research parallelizing the interior-row update on the available four-core ARM64 target, using the already-selected compiler/runtime configuration. Preserve a barrier before any copy or buffer swap, and combine per-thread convergence flags with a reduction equivalent to the source’s `hasError` test. Respect the harness’s thread budget and measure whether parallel overhead pays off; do not change the compiler or target.",
            "C": "Independently research a contiguous row-major allocation and vectorization-friendly stencil loop (for example, local non-aliasing row pointers). Keep the original neighbor addition order, update only the same interior cells, and retain the source’s convergence and output behavior. Do not combine this experiment with ping-pong buffers or threading in the initial candidate, so its effect can be measured separately.",
            "G": "Integrate the A, B, and C programs as measured candidates, not as assumed additive gains. Check standalone variants and useful combinations on the authorized correctness cases, including small sizes, zero or short iteration limits, and convergence. Preserve the original initialization, stopping semantics, and `%lf ` output format. Benchmark end-to-end on the specified target, retain only correct variants, and select the best measured integration even if it is a subset of the candidates.",
            "analysis": "The source performs a regular Jacobi stencil, checks convergence during the update, then makes a second interior pass to copy values; it also prints every grid value serially. This run’s baseline was correct on four cases, with mean time 0.0666652253518502 seconds and score 0.5; the target is ARM64 with four CPU cores and no GPU. These observations do not identify the dominant cost or predict an optimization’s gain. Keep every valid input correct, preserve the per-sweep arithmetic and convergence behavior, and verify end-to-end measurements rather than assuming gains combine."
