      "mother_plan:synthesis": {
        "model_calls": 1,
        "phase": "synthesis",
        "plan": {
          "A": "Research the low-memory streaming path. Refine the measured S-row ring-buffer approach (0.05453 s): read each matrix value once, maintain 64-bit vertical sums, and scan all horizontal windows. Test input and scan changes independently; retain the full-matrix implementation as a comparison. The measured tiled-transpose variant was slower (0.08441 s), so do not assume an extra transpose helps. Preserve correctness for negative values and every valid N,S.",
          "B": "Research the serial contiguous row-major path, focusing on input throughput and the vertical-update kernel. Compare a buffered integer parser with the current scanf path, and assess compiler-vectorized scalar loops before considering further hand-written SIMD: explicit NEON measured 0.05581 s versus 0.05573 s for the scalar candidate. Measure parser and kernel changes separately, keep intermediate sums 64-bit, and avoid transpose unless measurements justify it.",
          "C": "Research OpenMP work decomposition, using the measured top-row-block implementation (0.05420 s) as the control. Compare partitioning and scheduling of independent top-window ranges, and test a work-based threshold for avoiding parallel overhead on small workloads. The fixed column-chunk variant measured 0.05533 s, so treat it as an alternative to benchmark, not an automatic improvement. Use available cores without oversubscription, reduce maxima safely, and cover every window regardless of N,S.",
          "G": "Integrate complete candidates based on end-to-end measurements and correctness, not presumed additive gains. Start by comparing the fastest measured top-row-block candidate (0.05420 s) with the close ring-buffer candidate (0.05453 s); the serial contiguous candidate measured 0.05573 s. Test any potentially orthogonal change, such as buffered input, one at a time on the chosen base. Do not combine incompatible storage/work-decomposition approaches or add transpose/SIMD merely because they were explored. Retain only a correct integration whose repeated target measurements improve on its base.",
          "analysis": "The given serpentine traversal updates O(S) values per window move, giving roughly O(S(N-S+1)^2) work, while the measured rolling-sum candidates reduce the main work to O(N^2). Baseline mean time is 0.12576 s; the fastest candidate is 0.05420 s (about 2.32x faster). The leading candidates are close, and the transpose and explicit-NEON measurements provide evidence against assuming those changes help. The target is ARM64 with four CPU cores. Keep experiments general for all valid inputs, preserve 64-bit intermediate arithmetic and output correctness, and evaluate integrations as whole programs."
        },
