    "mother_plans": {
      "mother_plan:preparation": {
        "model_calls": 1,
        "phase": "preparation",
        "plan": {
          "A": "Research a serial algorithmic replacement for the per-bucket insertion sort. Compare a robust O(m log m) comparison sort over the existing string indices, preserving the baseline’s exact ordering and output format. Measure it independently of parallelism; check duplicates and uneven bucket sizes, and report correctness plus repeated timings using the existing harness.",
          "B": "Research parallel work across independent buckets on the existing four-core ARM64 target. Use correctly sized bucket storage (for example, counts plus prefix offsets) so skewed valid inputs cannot overflow the baseline’s equal-sized slices; then parallelize bucket sorting with bounded worker count and suitable scheduling. Compare against a serial robust-sort variant to isolate parallel benefit, including small-input overhead and load imbalance. Preserve exact output and do not change the target or harness.",
          "C": "Explore a stable bytewise radix sort of indices within each bucket as an alternative to comparison sorting. Process bytes from right to left and preserve strcmp-compatible ordering, including token termination where needed; retain the baseline bucket order and handle legal input lengths and distributions without test-specific assumptions. Validate against the oracle and measure independently before recommending it.",
          "G": "Integrate only programs produced by the research trees. First verify each candidate and any combined variant against the unchanged oracle on all supplied cases; then compare repeated end-to-end timings under the same compiler, target, and budget. Do not assume gains add: measure combinations, keep the fastest correct measured program, and reject any integration that regresses or changes semantics. Do not specialize to sample values or expected outputs.",
          "analysis": "The current run is for Problem D (Bucketsort): the observed baseline is correct on 4 cases, with mean 0.4240668 s, samples 0.4298925/0.4208170/0.4214909 s, and score 0.5 on a 4-core ARM64 CPU with no GPU. The supplied source uses insertion sort with strcmp within each bucket, making skewed buckets potentially quadratic; it also assigns each bucket a fixed size/nbuckets slice, which merits attention for skewed valid inputs. These support independent hypotheses—robust comparison sorting, parallel bucket work, and radix sorting—but there are no candidate measurements or profiler data, so none is a demonstrated improvement. Preserve the computation for every valid input; do not hard-code any size, string length, offset, bucket count, distribution, or answer."
        },
