    "mother_plans": {
      "mother_plan:preparation": {
        "model_calls": 1,
        "phase": "preparation",
        "plan": {
          "A": "Target the supplied Problem A source. Test pairing along the smaller matrix dimension, reducing band work from O(M·N²) to O(min(M,N)²·max(M,N)). For row bands, extend a column-sum vector and scan its prefixes; for column bands, retain the row-prefix approach. Keep the existing hash-based counter initially to isolate the orientation change. Preserve signed values and k, and the 64-bit result. Return a correctness-checked, measured candidate; do not assume a particular input shape.",
          "B": "Research the per-band prefix-frequency counter independently of band orientation. The current unordered_map performs repeated lookups and allocation/clearing work. Compare a reserved map with single-iterator lookup against a generation-stamped frequency array: prefix sums are bounded by ±1000 times the scan length, so use a derived offset and bounds-check s-k before indexing. Count prior prefixes before inserting the current prefix; retain signed arithmetic and a 64-bit answer. Compare actual time and memory, and return the best correct variant.",
          "C": "Test parallelizing independent band pairs with OpenMP on the 4-core ARM64 target. Each worker must own its frequency structure and 64-bit subtotal; combine subtotals without shared-map or counter races. Evaluate scheduling for the triangular pair loop and include parallel overhead in measurements; use at most the available 4 cores. Keep the serial path if measurements show parallel overhead outweighs the gain. First isolate parallelism from the other proposed changes.",
          "G": "Integrate only correctness-checked programs produced by A, B, and C. Measure each candidate with the same compiler, target, correctness checks, and timing procedure; compare with the baseline and retain the best measured version. Combine changes one at a time and remeasure each combination: independent speedups are not additive. Preserve a rollback candidate, exact output for all valid inputs, and the existing test/oracle/budget/target setup.",
          "analysis": "The supplied source and official statement are for Problem A, submatrix sums; B and C here are research-tree labels, not the official Problems B and C. The baseline is correct on 4 cases, with mean 0.2525351814 s (samples 0.2538497, 0.2518382, 0.2519176) and score 0.5. It pairs columns, scans rows, and uses an unordered_map for each band, giving O(M·N²) work. The target has 4 ARM64 CPU cores and no GPU. These data establish a baseline, not that any proposed optimization will win."
        },
        "request_sha256": "91bbbdf8f96a71e5ef3fb1463f0be9152e58367258dc671f80e18d0a6651aece",
        "response": {
          "data": {
            "A": "Target the supplied Problem A source. Test pairing along the smaller matrix dimension, reducing band work from O(M·N²) to O(min(M,N)²·max(M,N)). For row bands, extend a column-sum vector and scan its prefixes; for column bands, retain the row-prefix approach. Keep the existing hash-based counter initially to isolate the orientation change. Preserve signed values and k, and the 64-bit result. Return a correctness-checked, measured candidate; do not assume a particular input shape.",
            "B": "Research the per-band prefix-frequency counter independently of band orientation. The current unordered_map performs repeated lookups and allocation/clearing work. Compare a reserved map with single-iterator lookup against a generation-stamped frequency array: prefix sums are bounded by ±1000 times the scan length, so use a derived offset and bounds-check s-k before indexing. Count prior prefixes before inserting the current prefix; retain signed arithmetic and a 64-bit answer. Compare actual time and memory, and return the best correct variant.",
            "C": "Test parallelizing independent band pairs with OpenMP on the 4-core ARM64 target. Each worker must own its frequency structure and 64-bit subtotal; combine subtotals without shared-map or counter races. Evaluate scheduling for the triangular pair loop and include parallel overhead in measurements; use at most the available 4 cores. Keep the serial path if measurements show parallel overhead outweighs the gain. First isolate parallelism from the other proposed changes.",
