    "mother_plans": {
      "mother_plan:preparation": {
        "model_calls": 1,
        "phase": "preparation",
        "plan": {
          "A": "Research the matrix-multiply kernel in both overloads: improve row-major locality (for example, by transposing or tiling the RHS) and reduce the frequency of modular reductions with a proven overflow-safe bounded accumulator. Benchmark variants independently; preserve exact arithmetic modulo 1,000,000,007 and keep a serial path if a kernel change regresses small inputs.",
          "B": "Investigate replacing dense matrix exponentiation with linear-recurrence polynomial exponentiation. For the recurrence, use q(x)=x^N−P1·x^(N−1)−…−PN; compute x^(H−1) mod q by binary exponentiation and dot its coefficients with A1…AN. This offers O(N² log H) work rather than dense O(N³ log H). Validate the indexing, reduction, and modular arithmetic against the reference across valid inputs before recommending it.",
          "C": "Investigate parallelizing independent output rows or tiles of each matrix product under the fixed toolchain and resource limits. Cap workers at the target’s four cores, avoid nested parallelism, and use a measured size threshold with a serial fallback to account for launch and synchronization overhead. Do not change the compiler, target, or core budget.",
          "G": "Evaluate each tree’s candidate independently with the unchanged correctness and timing setup, then measure any combination rather than assuming gains add. Integrate only a candidate that remains correct and improves end-to-end performance; retain a general serial or baseline path when it wins for smaller runtime dimensions. Do not alter tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, permissions, or source files outside the authorized integration work, and do not specialize to test values or replace the computation with expected answers.",
          "analysis": "The supplied baseline is correct on 4 harness cases; its three samples average 0.0223418 s (reported score 0.5). There are no candidate measurements or prior role results, so the baseline is the only performance evidence and the test coverage shown is limited. The target is ARM64 with 4 CPU cores and no GPU. The source uses dense matrix exponentiation with repeated O(N³) products, a strided RHS access in the innermost loop, and a modulo after every multiply-add. The recurrence structure suggests a distinct O(N² log H) polynomial method; kernel locality/arithmetic and bounded multicore execution are separate avenues to investigate. Any gain, especially at this short baseline runtime, must be confirmed by the unchanged harness."
        },
        "request_sha256": "c9fd84d64d2323c4df83e544dc953e98af9007c2c329b4b465980992cf4e34dd",
        "response": {
          "data": {
            "A": "Research the matrix-multiply kernel in both overloads: improve row-major locality (for example, by transposing or tiling the RHS) and reduce the frequency of modular reductions with a proven overflow-safe bounded accumulator. Benchmark variants independently; preserve exact arithmetic modulo 1,000,000,007 and keep a serial path if a kernel change regresses small inputs.",
