            "C": "Study exponentiation overhead and parallel scheduling. In particular, avoid the final matrix square in the current power loop when that square cannot affect the result, and assess whether the returned matrix copy can be avoided. Benchmark serial versus OpenMP thresholds and thread settings against the measured fastest candidate; a separate four-thread parallel-region variant measured slower, so do not assume parallelism helps at every dimension.",
            "G": "Integrate candidates from A, B, and C, but measure each assembled program: their gains are not additive, and integration may be slower than its inputs. The baseline measured 0.022342 seconds; the fastest individual candidate measured 0.008872 seconds and passed four correctness cases. Use that candidate as a comparator, retain only verified improvements, preserve correctness across all valid inputs, and do not alter tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, permissions, or source files outside the assigned integration work.",
            "analysis": "The source uses dense O(N³ log H) matrix exponentiation for a recurrence with N≤1020 and H≤10⁹, making recurrence-specific O(N² log H) exponentiation a promising independent direction. Among measured candidates, SIMD dot-product chunks with static row parallelism measured 0.008872 seconds, faster than the measured row-tiled variants; another four-thread parallel-region variant was slower. The baseline measured 0.022342 seconds. Existing correctness evidence covers four cases, so it does not establish correctness for every valid input."
