    "mother_plans": {
      "mother_plan:preparation": {
        "model_calls": 1,
        "phase": "preparation",
        "plan": {
          "A": "Research a sparse-obstacle combinatorial solution. Sort and deduplicate blocked coordinates; precompute factorials and inverse factorials modulo 1,000,000,007 up to 2N−2. For each obstacle, compute paths reaching it minus contributions from earlier coordinate-wise reachable obstacles, then similarly compute paths to the destination. Handle blocked start/destination and duplicate points explicitly. Prove the recurrence and validate coordinate orientation against the source. Measure the O(M²) method across obstacle counts; do not assume it wins when M is large.",
          "B": "Research a memory-efficient dense-DP candidate. Replace the N×N blockedCells allocation and two-row DP with obstacle coordinates grouped/sorted by row and a single rolling DP row, preserving the exact start, boundary, and blocked-cell behavior. Target O(N²+M) time and O(N+M) memory. Explore a segmented prefix-scan within each row for 4-core parallelism, but measure synchronization and scan overhead against the serial rolling version; retain a correct fallback if parallel work loses.",
          "C": "Research parallel alternatives for the dependent grid DP, such as segmented row scans or tiled anti-diagonal wavefront scheduling. Specify dependency handling, obstacle representation, and memory needs before implementing; benchmark on the available 4 CPU cores (no GPU), including synchronization overhead and small-input regressions. Compare correctness and timings with both the dense baseline and other candidates; do not assume parallelism or a particular tile size helps.",
          "G": "Integrate only correctness-verified candidate programs from A/B/C. Benchmark each candidate and the integrated result on the harness workload, since gains may not combine and dispatch overhead can erase them. If measurements support it, use a general runtime choice between sparse combinatorial processing and compact dense DP based on N and M; select the crossover empirically, never from test-specific answers. Preserve valid-input behavior and output format, and report correctness plus measured timings.",
          "analysis": "The supplied source is Problem D: sequential O(N²) grid DP with an N×N blocked-cell structure. Official constraints permit N below 200,000 and can have sparse obstacles for large N, motivating both a sparse combinatorial candidate and a memory-efficient dense fallback. This run's baseline is correct on 4 cases; mean time is 0.081707686 s (samples 0.081716528, 0.081643922, 0.081762608 s), score 0.5. The target is ARM64 with 4 CPU cores and no GPU. No case dimensions or obstacle counts are provided, so do not infer the workload distribution or a crossover from these measurements."
        },
        "request_sha256": "7fdb2718b54c3e4c26fbe8fcc745d126f86822b5312fc1bb1ae5a709b17a8f81",
        "response": {
          "data": {
            "A": "Research a sparse-obstacle combinatorial solution. Sort and deduplicate blocked coordinates; precompute factorials and inverse factorials modulo 1,000,000,007 up to 2N−2. For each obstacle, compute paths reaching it minus contributions from earlier coordinate-wise reachable obstacles, then similarly compute paths to the destination. Handle blocked start/destination and duplicate points explicitly. Prove the recurrence and validate coordinate orientation against the source. Measure the O(M²) method across obstacle counts; do not assume it wins when M is large.",
            "B": "Research a memory-efficient dense-DP candidate. Replace the N×N blockedCells allocation and two-row DP with obstacle coordinates grouped/sorted by row and a single rolling DP row, preserving the exact start, boundary, and blocked-cell behavior. Target O(N²+M) time and O(N+M) memory. Explore a segmented prefix-scan within each row for 4-core parallelism, but measure synchronization and scan overhead against the serial rolling version; retain a correct fallback if parallel work loses.",
            "C": "Research parallel alternatives for the dependent grid DP, such as segmented row scans or tiled anti-diagonal wavefront scheduling. Specify dependency handling, obstacle representation, and memory needs before implementing; benchmark on the available 4 CPU cores (no GPU), including synchronization overhead and small-input regressions. Compare correctness and timings with both the dense baseline and other candidates; do not assume parallelism or a particular tile size helps.",
