        "plan": {
          "A": "Research per-node memoization of `get_costly` across requested start nodes, leaving lookup and input parsing unchanged to isolate the algorithmic effect. Cache by node-array index and preserve the source behavior exactly: choose the first neighbor with the greatest positive node value (ties keep the first), stop if none is positive, and add values in the same recursive order. Compare correctness and repeated timings against baseline.",
          "B": "Research faster `find_by_id` lookup in isolation, without changing recursive traversal or adding memoization. Use an index structure that supports arbitrary integer IDs; preserve the source’s first-match behavior if IDs repeat. Measure the lookup change separately and verify output formatting and correctness on all existing cases.",
          "C": "Research a DAG-based iterative precomputation alternative to recursive traversal. Select each node’s successor using the source’s direct-neighbor-value rule and original neighbor order, then evaluate in reverse topological order so each sum follows the same addition chain. Preserve missing-neighbor handling, float behavior, and output formatting; do not assume any particular graph shape beyond the stated DAG.",
          "G": "Integrate candidate changes only after reviewing their semantics and measuring them. Build and check correctness on the existing cases, then compare repeated timings of each candidate and the integrated program on the same target and conditions. Integration can regress despite individually promising changes: retain only a fully correct integrated revision with measured benefit, and reject regressions. Do not specialize to test inputs or alter tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, or permissions.",
