    "mother_plans": {
      "mother_plan:preparation": {
        "model_calls": 1,
        "phase": "preparation",
        "plan": {
          "A": "Research serial memory-traffic reduction. Compare a contiguous row-major allocation and double-buffer pointer swapping against the current row allocations and per-iteration interior copy. Preserve Jacobi updates: initialize both buffers with the source’s fixed boundary values, overwrite every interior cell before swapping, and output the active buffer. Check zero/negative iteration limits and small grids as well as normal cases; keep only a correct, measured improvement.",
          "B": "Research OpenMP parallelism on the stencil, using at most the target’s four CPU cores. Give each iteration a race-free change reduction (for example, integer OR), retain the synchronization needed before the convergence decision and next iteration, and use static work distribution. Measure serial and parallel variants, including overhead on smaller workloads; do not rely on a shared unsynchronized convergence flag or alter the iteration/convergence rules.",
          "C": "Research a serial, SIMD-friendly stencil and convergence check. Test conservative compiler vectorization and, if useful, a maximum-absolute-error reduction to replace the per-cell threshold branch. Preserve each cell’s arithmetic order, Jacobi semantics, strict threshold comparison, and output formatting; avoid fast-math/reassociation and in-place updates. Isolate changes so their performance can be compared independently.",
          "G": "Integrate only correct variants from A/B/C, first measuring them separately and then measuring the combined program: gains may not add, and integration can regress. Validate against the supplied correctness cases and preserve the source’s boundary, iteration-limit, convergence, and output behavior for every valid input. Use the available ARM64/4-core target, compare repeated timings with the baseline, and retain the fastest measured correct candidate rather than assuming an optimization helps.",
          "analysis": "The only automatic measurement is a correct baseline: mean 0.07135407160967588 seconds across three samples, with four correctness cases; score is 0.5. The target is ARM64 with four CPU cores and no GPU. There are no measured optimization candidates, so these are hypotheses to test, not established gains. The source’s two-pass iteration performs a stencil update and then copies interiors; its boundaries stay at their initialized values, and it stops when no interior update differs by more than 1e-5 or the iteration limit is reached. Those semantics constrain all proposed optimizations."
        },
