      "mother_plan:synthesis": {
        "model_calls": 1,
        "phase": "synthesis",
        "plan": {
          "A": "Research a contiguous row-major double-buffer layout combined with parallel row work and SIMD, to see whether it improves on B’s faster per-row kernel. Earlier contiguous candidates were slower than baseline, so test the layout only as a measured candidate; avoid repeating the padded-stride variant unless there is a specific new hypothesis. Preserve fixed boundaries and swap buffers only after a complete timestep.",
          "B": "Build from the best measured candidate: row-local restrict pointers, static OpenMP work over rows, SIMD within rows, and an integer-OR convergence reduction. Explore improvements to row-kernel vectorization or work distribution while retaining the exact stencil, strict convergence test, iteration limit, and timestep completion before swapping buffers.",
          "C": "Explore a persistent OpenMP team to reduce repeated parallel-region overhead. The measured persistent-team candidate was slower than B, so test whether using B’s integer-OR convergence criterion and careful synchronization improves it. All threads must make the same stopping decision, and buffer swaps must occur only after the full timestep completes.",
          "G": "Use B’s measured 0.04677 s candidate as the initial integration benchmark; it is faster than C (0.04961 s), baseline (0.07135 s), and the measured contiguous A variants (0.06779 s and 0.07019 s). Integrate A or C features only as complete candidates, then measure each integrated result and verify correctness: gains are not additive. Preserve behavior for every valid input, including fixed boundaries, convergence and iteration-limit semantics, and output formatting.",
          "analysis": "The source implements Jacobi iteration with fixed boundary cells, stopping when no interior change exceeds CONV_THRESHOLD or iter_max_num is reached. The strongest measured candidate combines row-local restrict pointers, SIMD, static row worksharing, and an integer-OR convergence reduction. The persistent-team and contiguous-layout experiments provide plausible alternatives, but their measured times do not beat that candidate as presented. Treat timings as evidence for prioritizing experiments, not as proof that feature combinations will improve performance."
        },
        "request_sha256": "6fd710a3b068a6339e12a37a1bd8ea91c47cf2b4259916a88e776639e4c032b6",
        "response": {
          "data": {
            "A": "Research a contiguous row-major double-buffer layout combined with parallel row work and SIMD, to see whether it improves on B’s faster per-row kernel. Earlier contiguous candidates were slower than baseline, so test the layout only as a measured candidate; avoid repeating the padded-stride variant unless there is a specific new hypothesis. Preserve fixed boundaries and swap buffers only after a complete timestep.",
            "B": "Build from the best measured candidate: row-local restrict pointers, static OpenMP work over rows, SIMD within rows, and an integer-OR convergence reduction. Explore improvements to row-kernel vectorization or work distribution while retaining the exact stencil, strict convergence test, iteration limit, and timestep completion before swapping buffers.",
            "C": "Explore a persistent OpenMP team to reduce repeated parallel-region overhead. The measured persistent-team candidate was slower than B, so test whether using B’s integer-OR convergence criterion and careful synchronization improves it. All threads must make the same stopping decision, and buffer swaps must occur only after the full timestep completes.",
            "G": "Use B’s measured 0.04677 s candidate as the initial integration benchmark; it is faster than C (0.04961 s), baseline (0.07135 s), and the measured contiguous A variants (0.06779 s and 0.07019 s). Integrate A or C features only as complete candidates, then measure each integrated result and verify correctness: gains are not additive. Preserve behavior for every valid input, including fixed boundaries, convergence and iteration-limit semantics, and output formatting.",
            "analysis": "The source implements Jacobi iteration with fixed boundary cells, stopping when no interior change exceeds CONV_THRESHOLD or iter_max_num is reached. The strongest measured candidate combines row-local restrict pointers, SIMD, static row worksharing, and an integer-OR convergence reduction. The persistent-team and contiguous-layout experiments provide plausible alternatives, but their measured times do not beat that candidate as presented. Treat timings as evidence for prioritizing experiments, not as proof that feature combinations will improve performance."
          },
