      "mother_plan:synthesis": {
        "model_calls": 1,
        "phase": "synthesis",
        "plan": {
          "A": "Research exact-size bucket ranges with a safe per-bucket sorting strategy. The stable mergesort candidate measured 14.302 ms, slower than the bucketed-qsort candidates, so compare it against qsort on the same inputs, including duplicate-heavy and skewed buckets. Preserve the source’s strcmp ordering and test boundary characters and tokens that share prefixes.",
          "B": "Continue evaluating radix sorting as an alternative, not as an assumed improvement: the cached-digit LSD variant measured 13.482 ms versus 14.437 ms for the other radix variant, but both trail the fastest bucketed-qsort measurements. Explore a bucket-local radix approach only if it preserves strcmp order for every valid token length; test ties and prefix cases, and avoid unsafe parallel histogram updates.",
          "C": "Refine the sharded-histogram, prefix-range, parallel-scatter, parallel-qsort design, which measured fastest at 13.029 ms. Audit and fix the shared outer `i` used by inner loops in the shown parallel regions; give each worker a private iteration variable, then remeasure. Use the 4-core target to investigate thresholds and scheduling under skewed bucket sizes. The serial-scatter/parallel-qsort candidate at 13.154 ms is a useful comparison.",
          "G": "Integrate conservatively: use the 13.154 ms serial-scatter/parallel-qsort candidate as a race-free reference, and consider the 13.029 ms sharded candidate only after correcting its shared-loop-variable race and remeasuring. Verify output correctness across valid inputs, especially bucket boundaries, skew, duplicates, and prefix-related tokens. Measure each integrated program as a whole; do not assume gains from combining the radix, mergesort, and parallel-bucket approaches will add.",
          "analysis": "This is Problem D: the source stores fixed-capacity string records, groups by first character, sorts each group with strcmp, then prints the strings. The baseline is correct on its reported cases and averages 0.42005 s; admissible variants measure 0.01303–0.01444 s, roughly 29–32× faster by those means. The fastest is the sharded C candidate, but its shown parallel loops share `i`, so its timing is not sufficient evidence of a safe implementation. The next-fastest C candidate uses serial counting/scattering and parallel qsort. Candidate correctness details are not included in the measurements, so verify correctness rather than infer it from speed."
        },
        "request_sha256": "a0ae9ec38f9d7c73c5749a170bf13844b9a4e05bf112e30c4da9381f0c8ff3e2",
        "response": {
          "data": {
            "A": "Research exact-size bucket ranges with a safe per-bucket sorting strategy. The stable mergesort candidate measured 14.302 ms, slower than the bucketed-qsort candidates, so compare it against qsort on the same inputs, including duplicate-heavy and skewed buckets. Preserve the source’s strcmp ordering and test boundary characters and tokens that share prefixes.",
            "B": "Continue evaluating radix sorting as an alternative, not as an assumed improvement: the cached-digit LSD variant measured 13.482 ms versus 14.437 ms for the other radix variant, but both trail the fastest bucketed-qsort measurements. Explore a bucket-local radix approach only if it preserves strcmp order for every valid token length; test ties and prefix cases, and avoid unsafe parallel histogram updates.",
            "C": "Refine the sharded-histogram, prefix-range, parallel-scatter, parallel-qsort design, which measured fastest at 13.029 ms. Audit and fix the shared outer `i` used by inner loops in the shown parallel regions; give each worker a private iteration variable, then remeasure. Use the 4-core target to investigate thresholds and scheduling under skewed bucket sizes. The serial-scatter/parallel-qsort candidate at 13.154 ms is a useful comparison.",
            "G": "Integrate conservatively: use the 13.154 ms serial-scatter/parallel-qsort candidate as a race-free reference, and consider the 13.029 ms sharded candidate only after correcting its shared-loop-variable race and remeasuring. Verify output correctness across valid inputs, especially bucket boundaries, skew, duplicates, and prefix-related tokens. Measure each integrated program as a whole; do not assume gains from combining the radix, mergesort, and parallel-bucket approaches will add.",
            "analysis": "This is Problem D: the source stores fixed-capacity string records, groups by first character, sorts each group with strcmp, then prints the strings. The baseline is correct on its reported cases and averages 0.42005 s; admissible variants measure 0.01303–0.01444 s, roughly 29–32× faster by those means. The fastest is the sharded C candidate, but its shown parallel loops share `i`, so its timing is not sufficient evidence of a safe implementation. The next-fastest C candidate uses serial counting/scattering and parallel qsort. Candidate correctness details are not included in the measurements, so verify correctness rather than infer it from speed."
          },
