    "mother_plans": {
      "mother_plan:preparation": {
        "model_calls": 1,
        "phase": "preparation",
        "plan": {
          "A": "Research asymptotic improvements to sorting and mode together. Replace quadratic bubble sorting with a stable O(rows log rows) method, optionally using insertion sort for small columns. Replace quadratic duplicate comparisons with a linear scan of sorted equal-value runs. Preserve stable ordering of equal values, including signed zeros, and the source's float conversion of the selected mode. Account for its float best-count comparisons rather than assuming unrestricted integer-count equivalence. Columns containing NaNs require separate, reference-compatible handling: bubble sort does not move values across NaNs, and equal values can then occupy separated runs. Do not exploit or reproduce uninitialized allocator contents. Document the mode ambiguity and validate the deterministic implementation against the unchanged oracle. Produce independently measured candidates showing whether both quadratic costs have been removed profitably.",
          "B": "Research independent-column execution and cache-local computation on the four ARM64 cores. After parsing, process each column through ordered sums, sorting, median/mode, sorted-order variance, standard deviation and coefficient of variation, keeping intermediates local. Compare this serial fused organization with coarse-grained column parallelism only if threading is supported by the unchanged build configuration. Use at most the available cores, private scratch storage and a serial path when parallel overhead outweighs useful work. Do not parallelize the existing uninitialized mode counters; use a defined, oracle-validated kernel. Preserve each column's reduction order, and keep output serial and ordered. Investigate multiplication instead of pow(deviation, 2) only with numerical compatibility and measured benefit; the compiler may already perform that optimization. Avoid floating-point reassociation and architecture-inappropriate intrinsics.",
          "C": "Research input, memory traffic and startup overhead, which may become dominant after algorithmic improvements. Compare the existing per-element fscanf calls with buffered tokenization using correctly rounded standard double conversion. Preserve accepted numeric syntax and values; do not introduce a restricted decimal parser or assumptions about positive, integral or bounded samples. Retain complete support for tokens crossing buffer boundaries. Experiment with accumulating arithmetic and reciprocal sums during row-major input while storing columns contiguously, maintaining the original addition order within every column. Evaluate buffering and allocation costs end-to-end rather than assuming fewer passes are faster. Keep all seven output lines, one-decimal formatting, trailing spaces and column order unchanged. Deliver parser/dataflow changes separately so G can assess their interaction with computation changes.",
          "G": "Integrate only programs produced and validated by the research trees. Establish one shared numerical and mode-compatibility contract before combining their column kernels, storage layouts or parsing changes. Preserve the fixed tests, oracle, compiler configuration, budgets, target and permissions; use only the existing measurement process. Compare valid standalone candidates with selected integrations end-to-end within the available budget. In particular, reassess threading after quadratic work disappears, and reassess input-time accumulation against column-local processing. Gains are not additive: keep the best measured standalone program if combinations regress. Require correctness checks for every integrated candidate, review general-input behavior beyond the four observed cases, and report any unresolved source/oracle ambiguity. Never dispatch on recognized test inputs, substitute expected answers or silently weaken numerical semantics.",
          "analysis": "The supplied program implements Problem B; A/B/C below are research roles, not contest problem labels. For r rows and c columns, bubble sorting and mode detection each require O(c*r^2) work, making their joint replacement the strongest structural opportunity. Input uses one fscanf per element and strided writes into otherwise contiguous columns. The only measured baseline passes four cases and averages 10.597 ms, with samples spanning 10.411–10.766 ms. No dimensions or phase timings are supplied, so bottleneck dominance and parallel speedups remain hypotheses; millisecond-scale overhead matters. The target has four ARM64 CPU cores and no GPU. Compatibility requires arithmetic and reciprocal sums in original row order, variance accumulation in sorted order using the original mean and division by r-1, and unchanged median arithmetic and formatting. Do not assume positive samples or replace exceptional arithmetic with convenient constants. A critical reference defect is that moda_aux increments uninitialized cont[i]; it also stores the winning value and count in float. Allocator-dependent output has no portable specification, and four passing cases cannot resolve this ambiguity. Research should use a documented, deterministic interpretation, retain defined observable behavior, and flag oracle disagreements rather than exploit undefined memory or change the oracle."
        },
        "request_sha256": "f5dee931ab48dbd345afe83ba6ae9bdd03f6eab6838c121a167cb7f14039c745",
        "response": {
          "data": {
